     Naturally, there's none.  Neither is there in dying as a result. 
Actually, however, I support the concept of realism.  I like the idea of
creating an alternate universe and making it follow a certain
(different) set of physical laws, with some similarity to our own (for
instance, people think roughly the same).  But, of course, there are
differences, e.g. magic and impossible creatures.  Naturally, realism
can get prohibitive to program.  But I do consider it an end to further.
For instance, I like that you can kick potions and they shatter, and
that you can throw them at monsters and they have the desired effect.  I
like that you can dilute potions in fountains.  Random effects of
fountains can be explained via the alternate laws the game runs by. 
Falling in a pool can't, at least as far as I can conceive.  Neither
can deciding whether to hit an undiscovered trap.

