     Alright, I'll bite.  I assert that the main focus of thought should
not be where you move next but on more long-term considerations.  The
exception to this is in combat, of course.  But I don't think moving
from here to there in a dungeon should be the main focus of play.  Now,
preparing a route to get from here to there more quickly is, for
instance to assure that monsters will find you while you're waiting for
them, or that you can get fresh sacrifices to altars.  How quickly you
go down levels is another strategy consideration.  Application of
objects I'd consider strategy-oriented.  Whether you want to be
invisible I'd call a strategic consideration.  Whether you're going to
eat an unidentified egg.  Whether you're going to eat while satiated in
order to gain an intrinsic (although I think there's a realism factor
involved here as well.  I'm not totally averse to surrealism, as long as
it's playable, though.  I'd note, however, that confirmation of choking
should be eliminated entirely if it's not always going to happen. 
That's deceptive.)  To fight or to run.  To let your dog fight and risk
that it dies, or to try to kill the monster yourself (I hate it when my
pet dies; no cursed object ID).  To convert a lawful altar to your
alignment in hopes of finding another deeper down, or to leave it for
holy water generation.  To attack that lawful priest in hopes it has a
Mjollnir or to get at its spellbooks, or to avoid risking death and (I
would guess) bad luck.  To pick up grey stones or not.
     What all these have in common is that you're considering the
ramifications of the action in question.  You're not simply trying to
avoid making an input error.  I consider it to be a strategic error
to die as a result of picking up a loadstone.  I consider it to be a
skill error to die as a result of picking up a known loadstone due to
the pickup option when you never intended to.
     Whether you want to fall in a moat or not is not something that
takes much thought.  The only reason someone would do that is due to a
lack of hand-eye coordination (or a slow/noisy system), i.e. skill.  And
I think such dangers are to be avoided.
